# AVA break-even calculator

A single-page calculator that finds the share of inbound voice calls AVA (the AI virtual agent) must contain before agent labor savings cover AVA's cost, and shows savings as containment grows. Hosted on GitHub Pages from `main` at the repo root: https://jchadcole.github.io/AVA-Break-Even-Calculator/

## Files

- `index.html`: the whole app. Plain HTML, CSS and vanilla JS in one file. No build step, no framework, no npm. The only external resource is Google Fonts. Charts and the flow diagram are hand-drawn SVG built in JS.
- `ava-break-even-check.xlsx`: validation workbook that recomputes the model with visible Excel formulas and has a Checks sheet. Generated by `tools/build_workbook.py` (openpyxl). Do not hand-edit the xlsx; change the script and rerun it.
- `customer-data-gathering.docx`: customer-facing fill-in form for every input, built by `tools/build_intake_form.js` (docx npm package). When an input is added, removed or renamed in `index.html`, update this form too. It must not mention our default values as assumptions; examples only.
- `.nojekyll`: keeps GitHub Pages from running Jekyll.

## Call flow (all percentages are shares of ALL inbound calls)

1. Direct to agent (`direct`, default 10%)
2. Contained in IVR (`ivr`, default 20%)
3. IVR → contained in AVA (`target` slider; this is "AVA containment")
4. IVR → AVA → agent = 100% − direct − ivr − AVA contained

Share reaching AVA `r = 1 − direct − ivr`. "% of AVA calls" = AVA contained ÷ r (vendor-style containment rate).

## Model (monthly basis)

- Handle hours per FTE = paid hours per month (`hours`, default 173) × (1 − shrinkage (`shrink`, default 30%, Chad 2026-11-11; industry norm 30–35% per Call Centre Helper, NiCE, Five9)) × occupancy (`occ`, 80%). Shrinkage = breaks, training, meetings, coaching, time off.
- Multi-task agents (Chad, 2026-11-11): `share` = % of working time on these calls (default 100%). It is NOT in the handle-hours denominator, because automating calls frees only call hours; the agents' other work remains. Putting it there would overstate FTEs saved by 1 ÷ share. `share` is used only in headcount mode (calls = agents × share × calls per FTE ÷ (direct + r)) and to show the whole team in calls mode (team FTEs = call-work FTEs ÷ share). All FTE results are FTEs of call work.
- Occupancy (default 80%; SQM gives 75–85% as the industry standard, with burnout risk above roughly 85–90%) should be the occupancy of time on these calls. It scales labor savings by 1 ÷ occupancy and applies equally with and without AVA.
- Calls per FTE per month = handle hours per FTE × 3600 ÷ average call time (`aht`, 600 s)
- Agent cost per call = fully loaded agent cost (`cost`, $7,000/FTE/month) ÷ calls per FTE
- Agent handle hours = (direct + IVR→AVA→agent) × calls × aht ÷ 3600
- FTEs required = agent handle hours ÷ handle hours per FTE; rounded up only when "Round FTEs up to whole people" (`whole`) is on. Whole people by default.
- Agent hours automated = AVA-contained calls × aht ÷ 3600
- FTEs saved = FTEs without AVA − FTEs at target (equals hours automated ÷ handle hours per FTE when fractional)
- Labor saved = FTEs saved × cost
- Billing rule: a call pays one automation fee. IVR-contained = IVR fee. IVR → AVA (contained or escalated) = AVA fee only, never IVR + AVA. Escalated calls add agent time.
- AVA per-call cost = AVA cost per call (`avaCost`, default $1.20) × (all calls reaching AVA if `chargeEsc` is on, default; otherwise only contained calls)
- AVA fixed = platform fee per month + implementation ÷ months
- IVR cost (`ivrCost`, $0.50): without AVA = (1 − direct) × calls × IVR fee (IVR-contained + IVR→agent); with AVA = ivr × calls × IVR fee (IVR-contained only)
- IVR fees avoided = r × calls × IVR fee
- Net savings = labor saved + IVR fees avoided − AVA per-call cost − AVA fixed = (cost without AVA) − (cost at target)

Comparison point: the same flow with no AVA, where every call leaving the IVR goes to an agent.
Break-even: the lowest AVA containment where monthly cost ≤ cost without AVA (found by scan + bisection in `model()`). Closed form with fractional FTEs: charged on escalations `c = (r × calls × (AVA fee − IVR fee) + fixed) ÷ (calls × agent cost per call)`; not charged `c = (fixed − r × calls × IVR fee) ÷ (calls × (agent cost per call − AVA fee))`; floor both at 0.

Display units (Chad, 2026-11-09): hours first, dollars beside. Every metric except the year-by-year chart shows agent hours as the main figure with the dollar amount in smaller text (`dual()` / `hrs()` helpers, `.usd` class). Dollar amounts convert to agent-hour equivalents at the agent cost per handle hour, `rate = cost ÷ handle hours per FTE` (default $67.44/hr). The monthly cost chart's y-axis is in agent hours. The year-by-year chart stays in dollars.

Year by year (`yearRows()` / `drawYears()`): for year y = 1…`years` (default 5, max 15), containment = min(r, target + `growth` × (y − 1)), with `growth` in points of all calls per year (default 5). Months in production = 12 − `implMonths` in year 1 (implementation time, default 3, 0–12) and 12 after. Net savings that year = months in production × (cost without AVA − (cost at that containment − implementation ÷ months)) − in year 1 only: implementation cost + `implMonths` × platform fee. So during implementation there are no savings and no AVA per-call fees, the platform fee still runs, and the implementation cost is charged once up front rather than amortized. Implementation payback on the target card = `implMonths` + implementation ÷ monthly savings (counted from contract start). The panel also shows a cumulative line and a table.

Volume input: calls per month is typed in by default (`mode: "calls"`). The "From agent headcount" toggle instead derives calls = FTEs today × `share` × calls per FTE ÷ (direct + r). The workbook mirrors both modes with a "Volume input mode" cell (1 = calls, 2 = agent FTEs) and computes "Inbound calls per month (used)", which every later formula references; a check confirms FTEs without AVA equal agents entered × % of working time on these calls in mode 2. Default-input results in mode 2 (100 agents, 100% on these calls): 72,660 calls, $732,697 without AVA, $398,397 net monthly savings, break-even 4.8%. With 75% on these calls: 54,495 calls, $549,523, $295,297, break-even 4.3%. Calls mode with 0% shrinkage and 75% on these calls: $694,200 without AVA, 58 FTEs saved, $358,960 net monthly, break-even 5.6%, $25,248,720 over 5 years.

## Assumptions to keep visible

- Labor cost scales with FTEs; agent time off these calls (breaks, training, other work) scales with headcount.
- Escalated calls keep the same handle time as direct calls.
- Default input values are examples, not confirmed customer figures.

## Conventions

- Keep it a single no-build `index.html`. Load any library only from a pinned CDN URL.
- All colors are CSS tokens on `:root` with dark-mode overrides; keep both themes working and the layout usable at 400px wide.
- Inputs persist in `localStorage` under a versioned key (`ava-containment-break-even-v9`; saved v8 and v7 values are carried over with shrinkage reset to the 30% default, and v7's old share becomes share = 100). Bump the version when defaults or the meaning of a field change so old saved values don't leak in.
- The year-by-year and monthly-cost chart panels are `<details class="fold">` elements that collapse; their open state is saved (`openYears`, `openCost`).
- The "Executive report" button (header) opens a two-page report overlay built by `buildReport()` from the current inputs. Each page is a `.rp-page` section sized 8.5in × 11in. Page 1 (`.rp-page.value`) is the executive value statement, kept short: a headline with cumulative savings over the chosen years, one plain-language sentence (containment, agent hours automated, % cost reduction, break-even), three big figures (annual savings at run rate, FTEs freed, payback), a bar showing break-even and target on a 0 to share-reaching-AVA scale, and three short points. Page 2 (`.rp-page.details`) holds the supporting details: break-even / target / hours / monthly cost tiles, the year-by-year chart (a clone of `#yearChart`) and table, the monthly savings build-up in hours and dollars, the call-path split, key assumptions and the method note. Payback = implementation time + implementation cost ÷ monthly savings. "Print / save as PDF" calls `window.print()`; the `@media print` rules (active while `body.report-open`) print only the two pages, one per US Letter sheet (`@page { size: letter; margin: 0 }`, page break after page 1). The yearly table switches to a `dense` style above 8 years so 15 years still fit. Keep page 1 uncluttered: new metrics go on page 2, and each page must still fit on one sheet.
- The "Show the math at target" panel prints each step with live numbers. Update it whenever a formula changes.

## Keep the workbook in sync

The workbook must stay zero-safe: guard every division ("Spread implementation over" uses MAX(1, months) like the page; rate, reach, calls and the closed form fall back to "n/a"), and check cells use ISNUMBER so text never reads as PASS. Zero inputs (fees, fixed costs, months, calls, agent cost, share reaching AVA) must recalculate with no #DIV/0! or #VALUE! anywhere.


Any change to inputs, defaults or formulas in `index.html` must be mirrored in `tools/build_workbook.py`, then regenerate the xlsx (`python tools/build_workbook.py`). With default inputs the page and workbook must agree: $974,200 without AVA, 83 FTEs saved, $33,600 IVR fees avoided and $533,960 net monthly savings at 50% AVA containment, break-even 3.7% (whole FTEs). In agent hours: 13,483 hr without AVA, 7,390 hr net monthly savings. Year by year with 5 years and +5 pts/year: with 3 months of implementation, $4,805,640 in year 1 (9 months in production) and $37,323,720 cumulative by year 5. Recalculate the workbook (e.g. open it in Excel) and confirm every row on the Checks sheet says PASS.
